Now make the painting in passes

This is where your idea gets much stronger.

Don’t classify a system once.

Paint the same scene repeatedly using different invisible variables.

Pass 1 — Attention

Who notices whom?

A \rightarrow \text{attention}(B)

Flower, spider, advertisement, infant crying, peacock tail, notification badge.

Suddenly wildly different systems rhyme.

Pass 2 — Desire

Why does each party care?

D =
\{\text{food, safety, mating, belonging, status, information, novelty, offspring, territory, meaning}\}

Same visible action + different desire = different system.

Pass 3 — Resource flow

Follow energy, money, nutrients, labor, time:

A \rightarrow R \rightarrow B

This often reveals the actual relationship underneath appearances.

Pass 4 — Information flow

Who learns about whom?

A \rightarrow I \rightarrow B

Signals, pheromones, language, prices, plumage, facial expressions, telemetry.

And sometimes the informational relationship is opposite the resource relationship.

A predator may give its prey information accidentally.

A flower deliberately broadcasts information to acquire a service.

Pass 5 — Control

Who changes whose behavior?

Not necessarily through force:

A \rightarrow \Delta P(B\text{'s actions})

A road controls cars.

A doorway controls humans.

A flower controls insect trajectories.

A user interface controls attention.

But B simultaneously acts back upon A.

Pass 6 — Bond

Ask what keeps two things together:

\text{force}
,\;
\text{reward}
,\;
\text{dependency}
,\;
\text{affection}
,\;
\text{identity}
,\;
\text{habit}
,\;
\text{shared enemy}
,\;
\text{shared offspring}
,\;
\text{shared environment}

Now you get a whole spectrum beyond predator/prey:

\text{stranger}
\rightarrow
\text{tolerance}
\rightarrow
\text{association}
\rightarrow
\text{exchange}
\rightarrow
\text{cooperation}
\rightarrow
\text{dependency}
\rightarrow
\text{integration}

At the far end:

\boxed{A+B \text{ cease behaving meaningfully as separate units}}

That’s how you get cells → multicellular organisms, or formerly independent organisms → obligate symbiosis.

Pass 7 — Time

Relationships mutate.

R_t(A,B)\neq R_{t+1}(A,B)

Competitors become allies.

Parasites can evolve toward lower virulence.

Parent/child becomes peer/peer.

Predator/prey can participate in ecological stabilization.

An attractor becomes repellent after satiation.

So relationship is a trajectory, not merely a label.

Pass 8 — Scale

This is where it gets weird.

At one scale:

A \text{ competes with } B

At another:

A+B \text{ cooperate against } C

Example:

Cells compete internally for resources, yet cooperate as an organism.

Individuals compete for status while their society cooperates against another society.

Species compete locally while jointly sustaining an ecosystem process.

Thus:

\boxed{\text{competition at scale }n
\text{ can produce cooperation at scale }n+1}

and vice versa.

Predator/prey dissolves when you move the camera.

