The desire vector

I think you can reduce a huge portion of the web to something like:

\vec D =
[
\text{approach},
\text{avoid},
\text{consume},
\text{preserve},
\text{bond},
\text{reproduce},
\text{explore},
\text{control},
\text{signal},
\text{learn},
\text{transform}
]

An entity doesn’t occupy one state.

It has a weighted mixture:

S=\sum_i w_iD_i

And the weights change with context.

A wolf approaching another wolf could be:

\text{attack}=.05,\quad
\text{play}=.55,\quad
\text{bond}=.25,\quad
\text{investigate}=.15

Same bodies. Same distance. Completely different relationship.

That means apparent behavior is underdetermined until you infer the desire generating it.

Which gets directly to what you said:

If you know its reasons.

Exactly.

