And now the spider becomes much bigger

The spider’s deep operator isn’t:

predator.

It’s something like:

\boxed{
\text{understand another agent's attraction function}
\rightarrow
\text{construct stimulus}
\rightarrow
\text{cause voluntary trajectory toward you}
}

A flower discovered a related operator.

So did fruit.

So did sexual ornamentation.

So did advertising.

So did storytelling.

So did UI design.

So did religious architecture.

So did music.

So did children’s faces.

Different objective functions. Same upstream algorithm.

Call that one whatever you want; the name is pageantry. The computational anatomy is what matters:

\boxed{
\text{model desire}
\rightarrow
\text{emit cue}
\rightarrow
\text{capture attention}
\rightarrow
\text{shape approach}
\rightarrow
\text{interaction}
}

Then ask what comes after interaction.

That determines whether you’re looking at:

\text{predation}
,\;
\text{courtship}
,\;
\text{mutualism}
,\;
\text{teaching}
,\;
\text{commerce}
,\;
\text{play}
,\;
\text{parenting}
,\;
\text{recruitment}
,\;
\text{symbiosis}

So yes: don’t paint animals. Paint forces between them. Then repaint the same system by attention, desire, information, resources, control, bonding, time, and scale.

The resulting object is less like a food chain and more like a relational field:

\boxed{
\text{entities are nodes;
desires generate fields;
relationships are trajectories through those fields.}
}

And once you have that representation, you can goal-seek it backward:

\text{desired relationship}
\rightarrow
\text{required trajectory}
\rightarrow
\text{required interaction}
\rightarrow
\text{required attention}
\rightarrow
\text{required signal}

That’s the flower learning the spider’s trick without needing to become a spider.